<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Workflow xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS COMMENT SITS INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT DELIBERATELY. A comment placed ABOVE
        <Workflow> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with a misleading
        "unable to find matching parent xml file" error.

        NEW FILE, 2026-08-19. Disposition__c had no workflow metadata before this change.

        🔴 THIS FILE CONTAINS [CORRECTED 2026-08-21: NO LONGER "EXACTLY ONE FIELD UPDATE". IT
        NOW HOLDS THREE. THE REST OF THIS SENTENCE STANDS UNCHANGED AND STILL APPLIES TO ALL
        THREE.] NO WORKFLOW RULE, AND THE ABSENCE OF
        A RULE IS THE POINT. Set_Approval_Advance_Pending is never fired by a criteria-based
        workflow rule. It is fired ONLY as a finalApprovalActions entry by three approval
        processes on this object:
            approvalProcesses/Disposition__c.Sale_Decision_Approval
            approvalProcesses/Disposition__c.Broker_Selection_Approval
            approvalProcesses/Disposition__c.Closing_Approval
        Do not add a <rules> element here to "activate" it - an active workflow rule writing this
        flag would fire a stage advance on an ordinary record edit.

        WHY A FIELD UPDATE ON A FLAG RATHER THAN ON Disposition_Stage__c ITSELF (design D-1):
        an approval FieldUpdate BYPASSES VALIDATION RULES ENTIRELY, so a direct stage write
        would let an approval skip the all-NDAs-signed gate and the wire gate. The flag is a
        SEMAPHORE: it re-fires DispositionTrigger, whose after-update handler detects false to
        true and enqueues DispositionApprovalAdvanceQueueable, which writes the stage with
        ordinary Database.update(..., false, AccessLevel.SYSTEM_MODE) - validation rules still
        evaluate, the approver's CRUD is irrelevant, and the recordEditability = AdminOnly lock
        has definitively released by the time the job runs. An approval-triggered FLOW was also
        rejected: with no runInMode it executes as the APPROVER, a measured incident on this org.
        The full reasoning lives in the XML comment on
        objects/Disposition__c/fields/Approval_Advance_Pending__c.field-meta.xml.

        ⚠ reevaluateOnChange IS false ON PURPOSE. It controls re-evaluation of WORKFLOW RULES,
        not of triggers. Triggers re-run on a field update regardless, which is exactly the
        mechanism D-1 depends on; turning this on would only add a workflow re-evaluation pass
        that has nothing to evaluate.

        ⚠ ONE UPDATE SERVES ALL THREE APPROVALS. It carries no information about WHICH approval
        set it - the Queueable derives the target from (record type, current stage). Approval
        history is where "who approved what" lives. Do not split this into three flags.

        DEPLOY ORDER - LOAD-BEARING. This file must land BEFORE the three approval processes: an
        approval process referencing a field update that does not exist fails the deploy. It
        must also land WITH or AFTER Approval_Advance_Pending__c.field-meta.xml.

        =====================================================================================
        2026-08-21 - TWO MORE FIELD UPDATES, FOR A DIFFERENT FIELD AND A DIFFERENT PURPOSE:
        Set_Approval_Pending AND Clear_Approval_Pending.
        =====================================================================================
        🔴 THEY WRITE Approval_Pending__c, NOT Approval_Advance_Pending__c. Two similarly named
        fields now live on this object and this file now writes BOTH. Read the disambiguation
        banner on objects/Disposition__c/fields/Approval_Pending__c.field-meta.xml before
        editing anything below; the short version is that the D-1 semaphore goes true on FINAL
        APPROVAL and the new flag is true only WHILE AN APPROVAL IS PENDING.

        WHAT FIRES THEM. The same three approval processes as the semaphore, but on all four
        transitions rather than one:
            initialSubmissionActions -> Set_Approval_Pending    (true)
            finalApprovalActions     -> Clear_Approval_Pending  (false, as a SECOND action
                                        alongside the untouched Set_Approval_Advance_Pending)
            finalRejectionActions    -> Clear_Approval_Pending  (false)
            recallActions            -> Clear_Approval_Pending  (false)

        🔴 CLEARING ON ALL FOUR TERMINAL TRANSITIONS IS THE WHOLE DESIGN, NOT BELT AND BRACES.
        The flag hides a Submit button, so a path that leaves it true hides that button forever
        on an unlocked record with no route to resubmit. RECALL is the path that has no other
        hook: a recall fires no rejection action, leaves no ProcessInstance status the record
        can see, and produces no trigger context. recallActions is the ONLY mechanism for it,
        and recall is a routine one click operation on this page since the Approval History
        component was added to Disposition_Record_Page on 2026-08-19.

        ⚠ A FIELD UPDATE IS USED HERE FOR THE SAME REASON THE SEMAPHORE USES ONE, PLUS ONE MORE.
        The reason above still applies (an approval triggered Flow with no runInMode runs as the
        APPROVER). The additional reason is specific to the pending window: recordEditability =
        AdminOnly LOCKS the Disposition for its entire duration, so no Apex at all can write
        this field while it matters. AccessLevel.SYSTEM_MODE does not lift a lock and neither
        does a Queueable, because a pending approval outlives the job. The approval process's
        own field update is the only writer that is not blocked.

        ⚠ AND WHY NOT SET THE FLAG IN APEX BEFORE Approval.process() INSTEAD. It would work, and
        it was rejected: DispositionService.initiateAndSubmit submits Sale_Decision_Approval on
        a route that never touches DispositionApprovalService, and the platform's generic Submit
        button is still reachable from list views, Classic, mobile and the API even though it
        was dropped from this object's Lightning page. initialSubmissionActions covers every one
        of those routes by construction.

        ⚠ ONE FLAG SERVES ALL FOUR SUBMIT BUTTONS ONLY BECAUSE THE FOUR ENTRY STAGES ARE
        MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE, exactly as the "ONE UPDATE SERVES ALL THREE APPROVALS" note above
        argues for the semaphore. A fifth approval sharing an entry stage would break the
        scoping silently. Do not split these into per approval flags without re reading that.

        🔴 MEASURED 2026-08-21: <description> ON A fieldUpdates ENTRY IS CAPPED AT 255
        CHARACTERS, AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF Clear_Approval_Pending FAILED VALIDATION AT 256.
        The org's answer is "Value too long for field: Description maximum length is:255".
        ⚠ THE CAP IS PER OWNING TYPE, SO DO NOT GENERALISE IT EITHER WAY. Measured in the same
        dry run: the CustomField descriptions in this change validated at 477 and 409
        characters, and the approval process files' own comments record descriptions of 442 to
        617 characters. WorkflowFieldUpdate is the tight one. Keep every description here
        comfortably under 255, not at 254.
        ⚠ AND THE FAILURE CASCADES MISLEADINGLY. One over length field update made all three
        Disposition approval processes fail with "references the workflow action FieldUpdate
        Clear_Approval_Pending, which doesn't exist for the Disposition__c object" - three
        errors that look like a missing element or a bad name and are neither. If you see that
        message, check the field update's description length before touching the approval files.

        reevaluateOnChange stays false on both, for the reason already given above: it
        re evaluates WORKFLOW RULES, and there are none in this file. Triggers re run regardless.
        Do not add a <rules> element to activate either one; an active rule writing this flag
        would hide a Submit button on an ordinary record edit.
    -->
    <fieldUpdates>
        <fullName>Clear_Approval_Pending</fullName>
        <description>Clears the approval pending UI flag. Fired by Sale Decision, Broker Selection and Closing approvals on all three terminal transitions: final approval, final rejection and recall. Clearing on all three stops a Submit button staying hidden.</description>
        <field>Approval_Pending__c</field>
        <literalValue>0</literalValue>
        <name>Clear Approval Pending</name>
        <notifyAssignee>false</notifyAssignee>
        <operation>Literal</operation>
        <protected>false</protected>
        <reevaluateOnChange>false</reevaluateOnChange>
    </fieldUpdates>
    <fieldUpdates>
        <fullName>Set_Approval_Advance_Pending</fullName>
        <description>Sets the D-1 auto-advance semaphore. Fired as a final approval action by Sale Decision, Broker Selection and Closing approvals; read and cleared by DispositionApprovalAdvanceQueueable, which writes the next stage.</description>
        <field>Approval_Advance_Pending__c</field>
        <literalValue>1</literalValue>
        <name>Set Approval Advance Pending</name>
        <notifyAssignee>false</notifyAssignee>
        <operation>Literal</operation>
        <protected>false</protected>
        <reevaluateOnChange>false</reevaluateOnChange>
    </fieldUpdates>
    <fieldUpdates>
        <fullName>Set_Approval_Pending</fullName>
        <description>Sets the approval pending UI flag. Fired as an initialSubmissionActions entry by Sale Decision, Broker Selection and Closing approvals. Four Dynamic Actions on Disposition_Record_Page hide their Submit button while it is true.</description>
        <field>Approval_Pending__c</field>
        <literalValue>1</literalValue>
        <name>Set Approval Pending</name>
        <notifyAssignee>false</notifyAssignee>
        <operation>Literal</operation>
        <protected>false</protected>
        <reevaluateOnChange>false</reevaluateOnChange>
    </fieldUpdates>
</Workflow>
